## Interface Modification by Ammonium Sulfamate for High-Efficiency and Stable Perovskite Solar Cells
Defects in perovskite films are still the dominant destroyer of both power conversion efficiency (PCE) and long-term stability in perovskite solar cells (PSCs). As the most popular electron transport layer (ETL), \(\mathsf{T i O}_{2}\) film is used in many PSCs to achieve high PCE. However, pristine \(\mathsf{T i O}_{2}\) by itself is not sufficient as an ETL due to lattice mismatch, poor alignment of the energy level gap, and hysteresis of the PSC. Herein, ammonium sulfamate (AS), with desired \(\mathsf{N H}_{4}^{+}\)and \(\mathsf{S}=\mathsf{O}\) functional groups, is designed to modify the \(\mathsf{T i O}_{2}\) surface and interface to improve the PCE of PSCs. It is found that the AS works like a seed layer for the perovskite deposition, and, in addition, it effectively forms a bridge between the \(\mathsf{T i O}_{2}\) surface and the perovskite. As a result, PSCs are successfully fabricated with a champion power conversion efficiency of \(24.78\%\) with smaller hysteresis. The PSCs prepared using the AS-modified \(\mathsf{T i O}_{2}\) also show excellent stability, and the bare device without any encapsulation retains \(96\%\) of its initial PCE after \(1056\,\mathsf{h}\) of ambient exposure at \(25\,^{\circ}\mathbf{C}\) and \(25\%\) relative humidity.
## 1. Introduction
To date, silicone-based solar cells have occupied most of the solar cell market and have maintained an untouchable position. However, silicon-based solar cells are still not suitable for some applications, such as space station solar cells, building integrated photovoltaics (BIPV), and wearable devices. Perovskite solar cells (PSCs), as robust candidates, have been undergoing development
from the lab to industrial scale over the past few years. In particular, the PCE of single-junction PSCs has been certificated over \(26\%\),[1–6] the PCE of perovskite-perovskite two-junction tandem solar cells is \(27\%\), and a PCE of \(32.5\%\) has been certificated for a perovskite-silicon tandem solar cell.[7,8] Despite the never-ending increase of the PCE of perovskite-based solar cells, some crucial issues still exist. Poor stability limits the application of PSCs, mainly their poor light, thermal, and encapsulation stability.[9–11] The mismatch of the crystallization process of perovskite film on the charge transport layers is another problem preventing high-efficiency and stable devices. For n-i-p structured PSCs, the commonly used ETLs include \(\mathrm{SnO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\). \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) is generally prepared by a simple and easily scaled-up chemical
bath deposition (CBD) method. So far, this kind of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) layer has been widely used as the ETL of perovskite modules, demonstrating good uniformity and superior performance. However, \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) film prepared by CBD has a different lattice constant and mismatch with the overlying perovskite polycrystalline film. At the interface, distortion and dislocation occur easily during the growth process of perovskite film.[12] At the same time, the conductivity and electron mobility of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) are relatively poor, and
the energy levels cannot match perfectly with the perovskite layer, which affects the electron transport and extraction from the perovskite films. Thus, various strategies have been used to solve these problems and correspondingly improve the PCE of devices.[13–16] Among these strategies, interface modification is the most popular method to improve the PCE of PSCs by reducing the energy barrier between the perovskite layer and the charge transport layer, improving the mobility of ETLs and reducing the density of pinholes in the perovskite films.
Hao Huang et al.[17] used tartaric acid (TA), ethylene diamine tetraacetic acid (EDTA) and phenyl phosphonic acid (PA) for ligand regulation during \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) deposition. The interface connected between the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) and perovskite layer was optimized, and the electron mobility of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) was improved. As a result, a \(24.81\%\) PCE was obtained for their PSC, which is the higher value for \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)-based planar PSCs. Zhenxing Li et al.[18] added single-layered MXene nanosheets during the process of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) deposition. The electron extraction rate, electrical conductivity, and surface infiltration of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) were improved, and the crystallization of perovskite film was promoted. Finally, a \(\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{AgBiBr}_{6}\) PSC with a PCE of \(2.81\%\) was prepared. Feng Gao et al.[19] prepared a PSC with a PCE of \(23.15\%\) by adding different alkali chlorides during the deposition of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\), which improved the conductivity and charge extraction ability of the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\). In addition, they adopted modification materials to modify \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) to achieve defect passivation and energy-level regulation. Hairen Tan et al.[20] found that chlorine-capped \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) \(\left(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}{\cdot}\mathrm{Cl}\right)\) was able to suppress deep trap states and reduce interface recombination of the PSCs, thus improving the PCE and stability of PSCs. Shuai You et al.[21] used heparin sodium (HS) to modify the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) layer, passivate the defects in the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) and perovskite film, reduce the hysteresis, and improve the environmental stability of PSCs. Mahdi Malekshahi Byranvand et al.[22] modified \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) with CuI, which changed the band arrangement and improved the conductivity of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\), resulting in the improvement of \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) of the corresponding PSCs. \(\mathrm{CeO}_{x}\), \([23]\) \(\mathrm{Na}_{2}\mathrm{S}\), \([24]\) poly(methyl methacrylate) (PMMA), \([25]\) \([\mathrm{BMIM}]\mathrm{BF}_{4}\) \(\mathrm{IL}\), \([26]\) and diammonium hydrogen phosphate (DAP) \([27]\) were also used as \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) layer modification materials to promote electron transport and improve the stability of PSCs. From the above studies, it can be seen that \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) modification is an effective way to improve the PCE and stability of PSCs.
Here, ammonium sulfamate (AS) was used as an interface material between \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) and perovskite film, which could play a triple role in passivation of defects, regulation of energy levels and improvement of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) electrical properties. To link the perovskite layer and ETL, the \(\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}\)and \(\scriptstyle\mathbf{S}=\mathbf{O}\) in the AS can interact with the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}\) in the perovskite layer, respectively. On the other hand, the energy levels of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) after AS modification are better matched with the perovskite layer, and the electrical properties of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) are also improved, both of which are more conducive to electron transmission. Theoretical calculation, Ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS), photoluminescence (PL), time-resolved photoluminescence (TRPL), transient absorption spectroscopy (TAS) and other tests have also proved this point of view. By optimizing the concentration of AS, the average PCE of the PSCs was increased from \(22.46\%\) to \(24.31\%\), and the champion PCE was \(24.78\%\). Under optimized conditions, a large-area (active area: \(1\,\mathrm{cm}^{2}\)) PSC with a PCE of \(22.90\%\) and a mini-module (active area: \(12.90\,\mathrm{cm}^{2}\)) with a PCE of \(20.37\%\) were prepared. After AS modification, the hysteresis index (HI) of the PSC was decreased, which is beneficial for sustained output capacity and stability of the PSCs.
