## 2. Results and Discussion
After 600s continuous illumination, the PCE of the PSC without the AS interface modification layer decreased to \(95.74\%\) of the initial value (from \(22.51\%\) to \(21.36\%\) ). In contrast, the PSC with the AS interface modification layer still retained \(98.83\%\) of the initial PCE (from \(24.04\%\) to \(23.76\%\) ). It is clear that the PCE and \(J_{\mathrm{mp}}\) with addition of the AS layer are higher than for the control device. Figure 1e shows the \(J{-}V\) curve of a large-area (1 \(\mathrm{cm}^{2}\) ) PSC prepared with the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) -AS layer, the PCE of \(22.90\%\) \((V_{\mathrm{oc}};1.143\,\mathrm{V},J_{\mathrm{sc}};25.75\,\mathrm{mA}\,\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) , \(\mathrm{FF};77.80\%)\) and \(22.11\%\) \((V_{\mathrm{oc}};~1.125~\mathrm{V},~J_{\mathrm{sc}};~25.65~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}\) , \(\mathrm{FF}:73.16\%)\) were achieved, respectively. Meanwhile, a perovskite mini-module (active area: \(12.90~\mathrm{cm}^{2})\) ) was prepared on \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) -AS layer, and its \(I-\) \(V\) curve was shown in Figure 1f. For the PSC prepared on \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) layer, we obtained a PCE of \(18.92\%\) with a \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) of \(6.40~\mathrm{V},\) a \(I_{\mathrm{sc}}\) of \(51.12\ \mathrm{mA}\) , a FF of \(74.02\%\) . However, a PCE of \(20.37\%\) was obtained by using PSC prepared by \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) -AS layer (corresponding to \(V_{\mathrm{oc}},\ I_{\mathrm{sc}},\) FF, and PCE were \(6.60~\mathrm{V},\) \(51.69\,\mathrm{\mA}\) , \(77.02\%\) , respectively).
The perovskite films prepared on \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) -AS were characterized by X-ray diffraction (XRD) and scanning electron microscopy (SEM). Figure 2a shows the XRD patterns of perovskite films prepared on \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) -AS with different concentrations. It is clear that no new peaks appear between \(10^{\circ}\) and \(60^{\circ}\) . The intensity of characteristic peaks in the perovskite-AS show a clear increase compared with the control sample. Figure 2b shows the amplified map of Figure 2a at \(2\theta=10{\cdot}18^{\circ}\) , where it is clear that the full-width at half-maximum (FWHM) obviously decreases and then increases as the AS concentration increases from 1 to \(3\;\mathrm{mg}\,\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) , and the FWHM reaches a minimum value when the concentration of AS is \(2~\mathrm{mg~mL^{-1}}\) . According to Scherrer, the corresponding grains should be the largest. Figure S2a–d (Sup
porting Information), show SEM images of perovskite film on \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) modified with 0, 1, 2, and \(3~\mathrm{mg\,mL^{-1}}\) c of AS, and the results of statistical analysis of their grain sizes are shown in Figure S2e–h (Supporting Information), respectively. It can be seen that the average grain size of the perovskite film on \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) modified with \(2\,\mathrm{mg}\,\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\) of AS is the largest, which is also consistent with the results obtained by XRD. The cross-sectional Field-emission SEM (FESEM) images of PSCs prepared on the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) layer and \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) -AS layer can be seen in Figure S3 (Supporting Information), which shows that the grains go through the whole perovskite layer, but the grain boundaries of the perovskite film prepared on the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) -AS layer are fewer and the grain sizes are larger. We also carried out atomic force microscopy (AFM) and Kelvin probe force microscopy KPFM on the perovskite films prepared on \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) film and \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) -AS film. The results are shown in Figure 2c,f. After \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) was modified by AS, the roughness of the perovskite film prepared on it was decreased (from 24.0 to \(18.7\,\mathrm{nm}\) ). This is more conducive to good Spiro-OMeTAD coverage. The KPFM results are shown in Figure S4 (Supporting Information). The surface potential of the perovskite film prepared on \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) modified by AS is increased (from 11.7 to \(21.8\;\mathrm{mV}\) ), which is more conducive to carrier transmission.
In order to study the effects of AS modification on the recombination and electron transport in perovskite film, we conducted PL and TRPL tests of the perovskite films prepared on \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) -AS films, and the results are shown in Figure 2d,e. The data were fit with curves of the form in Equation (1),[31,32] and the fitting data are shown in Table S1 (Supporting Information).
\[
f_{(\uparrow)}=A_{1}\exp\left(-\frac{t}{\tau_{1}}\right)+A_{2}\exp\left(-\frac{t}{\tau_{2}}\right)+B
\]
where \(\tau_{1}\) and \(\tau_{2}\) are the fast and slow decay lifetimes, respectively, and \(A\) is the amplitude. \(\tau_{\mathrm{ave}}\) is calculated from Equation (2).[33–35]
\[
\tau_{\mathrm{ave}}=\frac{A_1\tau_1^2+A_2\tau_2^2}{A_1\tau_1+A_2\tau_2}
\]
The lower fluorescence peak intensity and shorter carrier lifetime prove that the non-radiative recombination in the PSGs is reduced, and the electron extraction ability is enhanced by AS modification. Figure S4a,b (Supporting Information) show PL maps of perovskite films prepared on \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) -AS films, and they clearly show that perovskite films prepared on \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) -AS film have a lower carrier lifetime and more uniform distribution of average lifetime. This further reflects the improvement of the quality and uniformity of the perovskite film, which is attributed to the passivation of defects in the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) film and perovskite film by AS.
To investigate the effects of AS process on the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) surface, the surface passivation and electronic structure were studied by combining computational simulation methods. AS hydrolyzes easily and provides a large number of ammonium ion. Therefore, we
used anatase \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) (101) surfaces and \(\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}\) ions for modeling optimization. It was found that \(\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}\) ions on the surface of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) were decomposed into H ions and \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}\) , and then H ions were adsorbed and bonded with O atoms, as shown in Figure 3a. The surface passivation of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) by AS is actually due to the role of H ions dissociated from \(\mathrm{NH_{4}}^{+}\) ions. In addition, the partial density of states (PDOS) was calculated, as shown in Figure 3b, in order to study the influence on the electronic structure of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) before and after passivation. We can obviously see that the Fermi level of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) material moves toward conduction band minimum (CBM) after H ion passivation, and no impurity level is introduced in the bandgap. This indicates that N-type impurity forms on the surface of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) after passivation, which is a positive impact on electron transport and optimizes the role of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) as an electron transport layer in perovskite solar cells.
To study the electrical properties of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) films before and after AS modification, we employed the space charge-limited current (SCLC) method, where the carrier mobility can be calculated from the Mott–Gurney formula \((J_{\mathrm{D}}=9\mu\varepsilon\varepsilon_{0}\,V^{2}/8L^{3})\) .[36,37] The results in Figure 3c show that the electron mobility of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) -AS film
\((2.274 \times 10^{-3} \, \mathrm{cm}^{2} \, \mathrm{V}^{-1} \, \mathrm{S}^{-1})\) is higher than that of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) film (1.069 \(\times 10^{-3} \, \mathrm{cm}^{2} \, \mathrm{V}^{-1} \, \mathrm{S}^{-1}\)), which also indicates that using \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)-AS as the ETL of PSCs is more conducive to electron transport. The conductivities of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)-AS films were determined from Equation (3).[38,39]
\[
I=\sigma Ad^{-1}V
\]
where \(A\) is the device area, and \(d\) is the thickness of the ETL. According to the \(I{-}V\) curve of Figure 3d, the conductivity of the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) film was \(2.772 \times 10^{-3} \, \mathrm{mS \, cm}^{-1}\) and was significantly increased to \(5.959 \times 10^{-3} \, \mathrm{mS \, cm}^{-1}\) after AS modification. This is also consistent with the results of our theoretical calculation.
Ultrafast transient absorption spectroscopy (TAS) was also used to study the effect of AS-modified \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) on electron transport. TAS was carried out on device structures of Glass/Perovskite, \(\mathrm{FTO/TiO}_{2}\)/Perovskite and \(\mathrm{FTO/TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{AS}/\) Perovskite, and the results in Figure \(3\mathrm{e}{-}\mathrm{g}\) show that ground-state bleaching appears at \({\approx}790\,\mathrm{nm}\) . The peak intensity can reflect the number of carriers in the perovskite conduction band and valence band. Compared with the films in the Glass/Perovskite and \(\mathrm{FTO/TiO}_{2}\)/Perovskite structures, the ground-state bleaching of the film in the \(\mathrm{FTO/TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{AS}/\) Perovskite structure is weaker, which is because the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) modified by AS is more conducive to electron transport. Figure 3h shows the decay dynamics of perovskite films with the three different structures. The \(\tau_{\mathrm{ave}}\) of Glass/Perovskite, \(\mathrm{FTO/TiO}_{2}\)/Perovskite and \(\mathrm{FTO/TiO}_{2}/\mathrm{AS}/\) Perovskite can be abbreviated as \(\tau_{\mathrm{ave-T}},\,\tau_{\mathrm{ave-P}}\) and \(\tau_{\mathrm{ave-P-A}}\) , respectively. According to the literature, the electron injection rate \((k_{\mathrm{ET}})\) can be calculated by Equation (4).[40]
\[
k_{\mathrm{ET}}=\frac{1}{\tau_{\mathrm{ET}}}=\frac{1}{\tau_{\mathrm{ave-T}}}\text{or}\frac{1}{\tau_{\mathrm{ave-T-A}}}-\frac{1}{\tau_{\mathrm{ave-P}}}
\]
The obtained \(k_{\mathrm{ET}}\) values of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)-AS are \(1.63 \times 10^{-2}\) \(\mathrm{ns}^{-1}\) and \(3.89 \times 10^{-2} \, \mathrm{ns}^{-1}\), respectively. Obviously, \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) has stronger electron extraction ability after AS modification, which is also consistent with the PL and TRPL test results.
In order to study the interaction between AS and \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\), we conducted X-ray photoemission spectroscopy (XPS) tests on \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)-AS film. Figure 4a–c are XPS Spectra of Ti 2p, S 2p and O 1s, respectively, for \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)-AS films. The Ti 2p spectrum for the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) film displays two distinct peaks at 464.54 and \(458.78 \, \mathrm{eV}\) corresponding to Ti 2p1/2 and \(2\mathrm{p}3/2\), respectively. But for \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)-AS film, the peaks of Ti 2p1/2 and \(2\mathrm{p}3/2\) are at 464.75 and \(458.99 \, \mathrm{eV}\). For the O 1s peaks of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) film and \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)-AS film, there is also a shift (from 530.11 to \(530.32 \, \mathrm{eV}\)). Compared with the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) film, these shifts of the peak positions of Ti 2p and O1s of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)-AS film indicate that there is an interaction between \(-\mathrm{NH}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\). As can be seen in Figure 4b, the peak position of S 2p was detected only for the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)-AS film, indicating that AS still existed on the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) surface after annealing. To confirm the interaction between \(\mathrm{S}=\mathrm{O}\) bonds in AS and \(\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}\) in the perovskite layer, we obtained the FTIR spectra of AS and \(\mathrm{AS}+\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\), and the results are shown in Figure 4d. According to the literature,[41–43] the vibration peaks of \(\mathrm{S}=\mathrm{O}\) in AS are at \(\approx 1056\), 1100, and \(1210 \, \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\), but the vibration peaks of \(\mathrm{S}=\mathrm{O}\) in \(\mathrm{AS}+\mathrm{PbI}_{2}\) were located at 1031, 1054, and \(1250 \, \mathrm{cm}^{-1}\). The \(\mathrm{S}=\mathrm{O}\) peak displacement indicates that
the \(\mathrm{S}=\mathrm{O}\) interacts with \(\mathrm{Pb}^{2+}\) and can passivate the anion defects in the perovskite film.
In order to study whether \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) modified by AS would affect the light absorption of the perovskite layer, we conducted UV–vis spectroscopy of perovskite films prepared on \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)-AS, and the results in Figure 4e show that AS modification does not affect the light transmission, which also indicates that the bandgap of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) does not change after AS modification. According to the transmittance, the bandgap of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) is \(3.2 \, \mathrm{eV}\), as shown in Figure 4f. Similarly, the absorption of the perovskite film was measured, and the result is shown in Figure S6a (Supporting Information). The bandgap of the perovskite film was \(1.53 \, \mathrm{eV}\) (Figure S6b, Supporting Information). To study the changes in \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) energy levels, UPS was carried out on \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) film and \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)-AS film, and the results are shown in Figure 4g. The energy levels of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) were obtained and used to create the energy level diagram shown in Figure 4h. UPS was also performed to characterize the perovskite film, and the result is shown in Figure S5c (Supporting Information). The conduction and valence band positions of the perovskite film can also be obtained. Finally, the energy level positions of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\), \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)-AS, and perovskite films were obtained, as shown in Figure 4i. It can be seen that after AS modification, the conduction band and Fermi level of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) moved up, which is more conducive to the transmission of electrons and can effectively reduce the internal recombination of PSCs. This is also one of the reasons for the high PCE of PSCs prepared using \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\)-AS as the ETL. At the same time, the results obtained by UPS test are also consistent with our theoretical calculation results.
\(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) -AS layer has a lower \(V_{\mathrm{TFL}}\) (0.11 V), which indicates that AS has the ability to passivate defects.
In addition, we also conducted electrochemical spectroscopy (EIS) on cells prepared using \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) -AS, and the results are shown in Figure 5c, with the inset showing the equivalent circuit. The fitted data are shown in Table S2 (Supporting Information). Compared with the PSCs prepared with \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) , the PSCs prepared with \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) -AS have smaller \(R_{\mathrm{s}}\) , larger \(R_{\mathrm{tr}},\) and smaller \(\mathrm{R}_{\mathrm{rec}}\) . This is because AS-modified \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) promoted electron transport, reduced the defect density, and improved the contact between the perovskite layer and the electron transport layer and hole transport layer, which is consistent with the UPS and AFM results. Capacitance–voltage \((C{-}V)\) testing of the PSCs prepared with \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) -AS was also performed, and the results are shown in Figure 5d. The built-in potential \(\left(V_{\mathrm{bi}}\right)\) can be extracted according to the Mott–Schottky equation.[49–51] The PSC prepared with \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) -AS has a higher \(V_{\mathrm{bi}}\) , which is also the reason for the improvement of \(V_{\mathrm{oc}}\) , and it is also consistent with the UPS results.
The environmental stability of PSCs prepared with \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) and \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) -AS was tested, and the results are shown in Figure 5e. The relative humidity was \({\approx}25\%\) , the temperature was \({\approx}25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C},\) and the PSCs had no encapsulation. After \(1056\,\mathrm{h}\) , the average PCE of PSCs prepared using \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) -AS maintained \({\approx}96\%\) of the initial PCE (decaying from 24.10 to \(23.12\%\) ); however, the average PCE of the PSCs prepared with \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) was only \({\approx}71\%\) of the initial PCE (decaying from \(22.67\%\) to \(16.04\%\) ). The improvement of the PSC stability is also attributed to the passivation of anion defects in the perovskite film by AS.
## 3. Conclusion
In summary, we introduce an interface layer (AS) between the \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) layer and the perovskite layer of the PSCs. The AS layer mainly plays the following roles: 1) The \(\mathrm{NH}_{4}^{+}\) group in AS passivated the defects on the surface of \(\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\) , reducing the defects in PSCs; 2) The energy level between the perovskite layer and
\( \mathrm{TiO}_{2} \) was better matched, which can promote electron transport in the PSCs; 3) Improve the electrical properties (conductivity and electron mobility) of \( \mathrm{TiO}_{2} \) to facilitate electron transport in PSCs; 4) The \( \mathrm{S}=\mathrm{O} \) group in AS interacted with the \( \mathrm{Pb}^{2+} \) in the perovskite layer to passivate anion defects and improve the PCE and stability of PSCs. By optimizing the concentration of AS, the average PCE of PSCs was increased from \( 22.46\% \) to \( 24.31\% \), and the champion PCE of \( 24.78\% \) was achieved. Using AS-modified \( \mathrm{TiO}_{2} \), we also prepared a large-area \( (1\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}) \) and a PSC mini-module \( (12.90\ \mathrm{cm}^{2}) \), the PCE were reached \( 22.90\% \) and \( 20.37\% \), respectively. At the same time, the continuous output capacity and environmental stability of PSCs prepared with \( \mathrm{TiO}_{2} \) films modified by AS has also been greatly improved. This research lays the foundation for interface modification, preparation of large-area PSCs, and further progress toward commercialization of PSCs.
